import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public List<Integer> findAnagrams(String s, String p) {
    List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
    if (s.length() < p.length()) return result;

    int[] s_count = new int[26];
    int[] p_count = new int[26];
    for (char c : p.toCharArray()) p_count[c - 'a']++;

    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
        s_count[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        if (i >= p.length()) s_count[s.charAt(i - p.length()) - 'a']--;
        if (java.util.Arrays.equals(s_count, p_count)) result.add(i - p.length() + 1);
    }

    return result;
}
```

    1. Create an empty result list.
2. If the given string `s` is shorter than the pattern string `p`, return the empty result list as there cannot be any anagram of `p` in `s`.
3. Create 2 arrays, `s_count` and `p_count`, with a fixed size of 26, to keep count of characters in the sliding window of `s` and characters in `p`.
4. Fill `p_count` using the characters count in `p`.
5. Iterate over the string `s`, updating the count of the characters in `s_count`.
6. If the iteration index `i` is greater than or equal to the length of `p`, reduce the count of the character that is now out of the sliding window.
7. If the `s_count` and `p_count` have the same counts, it means that a valid anagram has been found. Add the starting index of the anagram in the result list.
8. Return the result list.

