public boolean checkInclusion(String s1, String s2) {
    if (s1.length() > s2.length()) return false;
    int[] s1_map = new int[26];
    int[] s2_map = new int[26];

    for (int i = 0; i < s1.length(); i++) {
        s1_map[s1.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        s2_map[s2.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < s2.length() - s1.length(); i++) {
        if (Arrays.equals(s1_map, s2_map)) return true;
        s2_map[s2.charAt(i) - 'a']--;
        s2_map[s2.charAt(i + s1.length()) - 'a']++;
    }

    return Arrays.equals(s1_map, s2_map);
}
```


    1. We maintain two array s1_map and s2_map to keep track of the character frequency in the respective strings.
2. If the length of s1 is larger than s2 then return false.
3. Iterate through the length of s1 and increment the count for each character in both s1_map and s2_map.
4. Now iterate through length of s2 - length of s1 to keep the sliding window of size s1.
5. If s1_map and s2_map are equal during the iterations, return true.
6. Otherwise, slide the window by updating the counts in s2_map.

The algorithm will return the result once the iterations are completed.

