1. Initialize `maxLength` and `ans` to `0`.
2. Initialize the `length` and `count` arrays to all have a value of `1`.
3. Loop through the `nums` array using variable `i`.
    a. Loop from `0` to `i - 1` using a variable `j`.
        i. If `nums[i] > nums[j]`, update the values of `length[i]` and `count[i]` based on their current values and the values at index `j`. The rule for this is:
            * If `length[i] == length[j] + 1`, increase `count[i]` by `count[j]`.
            * If `length[i] < length[j] + 1`, update `length[i]` to `length[j] + 1` and update `count[i]` to `count[j]`.
    b. If `maxLength` is equal to `length[i]`, increment `ans` by `count[i]`. If `maxLength` is less than `length[i]`, update `maxLength` to equal `length[i]` and update `ans` to equal `count[i]`.
4. Return `ans` as the final result.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2) and a space complexity of O(n).

