public int longestMountain(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    int maxLength = 0;
    int i = 1;
    while (i < n - 1) {
        if (arr[i - 1] < arr[i] && arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
            int left = i - 1;
            int right = i + 1;
            while (left > 0 && arr[left - 1] < arr[left]) {
                left--;
            }
            while (right < n - 1 && arr[right] > arr[right + 1]) {
                right++;
            }
            maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, right - left + 1);
            i = right;
        } else {
            i++;
        }
    }
    return maxLength;
}
```

    1. Initialize `max_length` to zero and `i` to 1. We'll use it to iterate through the input array `arr`.
2. Use a while loop to iterate through the array until we reach the second last element.
   - Check if the current element `i` is a peak (i.e., `arr[i-1] < arr[i] > arr[i+1]`).
   - If it's a peak, find the left and right boundaries of the mountain by:
       - Iterate left as long as the slope keeps ascending (`arr[left - 1] < arr[left]`).
       - Iterate right as long as the slope keeps descending (`arr[right] > arr[right + 1]`).
   - When both left and right boundaries are found, calculate the mountain length (`right - left + 1`) and compare it to the current `max_length`. Update `max_length` if the new mountain is bigger.
   - Move `i` to the right boundary and continue the loop.
   - If it's not a peak, just increment `i`.
3. Return the `max_length` of the mountains.

