import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public String lexSmallestEquivalent(String s1, String s2, String baseStr) {
    Map<Character, Character> parent = new HashMap<>();
    for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
        parent.put(ch, ch);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < s1.length(); i++) {
        char root1 = s1.charAt(i), root2 = s2.charAt(i);
        while (parent.get(root1) != root1) root1 = parent.get(root1);
        while (parent.get(root2) != root2) root2 = parent.get(root2);
        if (root1 != root2) {
            parent.put(root1, (char) Math.min(root1, root2));
            parent.put(root2, (char) Math.min(root1, root2));
        }
    }

    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (char ch : baseStr.toCharArray()) {
        while (parent.get(ch) != ch) ch = parent.get(ch);
        sb.append(ch);
    }

    return sb.toString();
}
```

    - Create a dictionary/ HashMap/ Map, called `parent`, where the key is the English letter and the value is the letter itself.
- Iterate through the `s1` and `s2`: for every character in `s1` and `s2`, find the roots of these characters in the `parent` dictionary/ HashMap/ Map.
- If the roots are not equal, set the value of each root in the dictionary as the minimum of the roots.
- Then, iterate through the `baseStr` characters: for each character, find its root in the parent dictionary and replace the character with its root.
- Join the baseStr characters back to a string and return it.

