public int maxSumSubsequence(int[] nums, int k) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int[] dp = new int[n];
    int result = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        dp[i] = nums[i];
        for (int j = Math.max(0, i - k); j < i; ++j) {
            dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + nums[i]);
        }
        result = Math.max(result, dp[i]);
    }

    return result;
}
```

    The algorithm creates a dynamic programming (DP) array `dp` with the same size as the input array `nums`. This DP array will store the maximum sum subsequence ending at each index. It initializes the maximum sum to 0, which needs to be updated during the dynamic programming process.

For each index `i` from 0 to n-1, it sets the value in the DP array as the value of `nums[i]`. Then, it checks all possible previous indices within the range of `k`, which indicates the maximum gap between the two consecutive elements in the subsequence. For each index `j`, it updates the DP value at the current index `i` as the maximum of the current DP value and the sum of the value of `nums[i]` and the previous value `dp[j]`.

Finally, it keeps track of the maximum sum found during this process in a variable `result`, and returns it after looping through the whole array.

