public int sumOfFlooredPairs(int[] nums) {
    int mod = 1000000007;
    int maxVal = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    for (int num : nums) {
        maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, num);
    }
    int[] count = new int[maxVal + 1];
    long[] prefSum = new long[maxVal + 1];

    for (int num : nums) {
        count[num]++;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= maxVal; i++) {
        prefSum[i] = (prefSum[i - 1] + count[i]) % mod;
    }

    long res = 0;
    for (int x = 1; x <= maxVal; x++) {
        for (int k = 1, nx = x; nx <= maxVal; k++, nx += x) {
            res = (res + ((prefSum[nx] - prefSum[nx - x] + mod) % mod) * k % mod * count[x] % mod) % mod;
        }
    }
    return (int) res;
}
```

    The algorithm first calculates the maximum value present in the array named maxVal. Then it initializes two other arrays: count and prefSum of size (maxVal + 1). The count array stores the frequency count of numbers in the input array, and prefSum stores the prefix sum of count array modulo mod (10^9 + 7).

The main logic is implemented using nested loops. The outer loop iterates through each unique number (x) in the input array, and the inner loop generates multiples of that number (nx) within the range of the maximum value. For each nx, calculate the result as the product of count[x], k, and the sum of the prefSum values within the range (nx - x) and nx modulo mod. The final result is obtained by summing these products modulo mod for every possible pair of numbers in the input array.

The time complexity of the algorithm is O(maxVal^2) where maxVal is the maximum value in the input array nums. The space complexity is O(maxVal) due to the count and prefSum arrays.

