public int minDifficulty(int[] jobDifficulty, int d) {
    int n = jobDifficulty.length;
    if (n < d) return -1;
    int[][] dp = new int[d][n];

    dp[0][0] = jobDifficulty[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        dp[0][i] = Math.max(dp[0][i-1], jobDifficulty[i]);
    }

    for (int i = 1; i < d; ++i) {
        for (int j = i; j < n; ++j) {
            int maxD = jobDifficulty[j];
            for (int k = j; k >= i; --k) {
                maxD = Math.max(maxD, jobDifficulty[k]);
                dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], dp[i-1][k-1] + maxD);
            }
        }
    }
    return dp[d-1][n-1];
}
```


    We use a dynamic programming approach to solve this problem. The `dp[i][j]` represents the minimum difficulty of a job schedule for the first `j+1` jobs in `i+1` days. We first initialize the difficulty for the first day. Then, for each day from 1 to `d - 1`, we loop through each job `j` starting from the current day index `i`. We find the maximum difficulty for each day by iterating through the jobs in reverse order (from the last job to the start of the day) and maintain a temporary maximum difficulty during this iteration. Finally, we find the minimum of the current difficulty at `dp[i][j]` and the previous day's difficulty plus the temporary maximum difficulty.

After the nested loops, the result can be retrieved from `dp[d-1][n-1]`.

