public int maxDotProduct(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
    int n = nums1.length, m = nums2.length;
    int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][m + 1];
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
        Arrays.fill(dp[i], Integer.MIN_VALUE);
    }
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
            dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i][j], Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], Math.max(dp[i][j - 1], Math.max(dp[i - 1][j - 1] + nums1[i - 1] * nums2[j - 1], nums1[i - 1] * nums2[j - 1]))));
        }
    }
    return dp[n][m];
}
```

    The algorithm iterates over both input arrays with nested loops, creating a two-dimensional DP table. For each cell (i, j) in the table, it finds the maximum between multiple alternatives:
1. Previous value at dp[i][j]
2. Value at dp[i-1][j]
3. Value at dp[i][j-1]
4. Value at (dp[i-1][j-1] + nums1[i-1] * nums2[j-1]) (i.e., including a dot product at the current position)
5. Value at (nums1[i-1] * nums2[j-1]) (i.e., starting a new subsequence at the current position)

After filling the DP table, dp[n][m] contains the maximum dot product between the two input arrays.

