public int numSubmat(int[][] mat) {
    int m = mat.length, n = mat[0].length;
    int[][] dp = new int[m][n];
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (mat[i][j] == 1) {
                dp[i][j] = (j == 0) ? 1 : dp[i][j - 1] + 1;
                int minWidth = dp[i][j];
                for (int k = i; k >= 0; k--) {
                    minWidth = Math.min(minWidth, dp[k][j]);
                    count += minWidth;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```

    1. Create a DP matrix of the same dimensions as the given binary matrix, and a `count` variable.
2. Loop through each cell of the binary matrix. If the cell contains a 1, update the DP matrix cell with either 1 if it's the first column, or add 1 to the value of the previous column in the same row.
3. For each cell with a 1, iterate from its row to the first row, calculating the minimum width found in the DP matrix, and update the count with the minimum width.
4. The final count value will be the number of submatrices containing all ones.

