import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public int maxOperations(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
    int operations = 0;

    for (int num : nums) {
        if (count.getOrDefault(k - num, 0) > 0) {
            count.put(k - num, count.get(k - num) - 1);
            operations++;
        }
        else {
            count.put(num, count.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
        }
    }

    return operations;
}
```


    The algorithm uses a hashmap (called "count") to store the count of each number in the array. For each number in the array, it checks if there is a complementary number, i.e., if the difference, `k - number`, is present in the hashmap with count greater than 0.

If found, it decrements the count and increments the operations performed. If not, it increments the count of the number in the hashmap. This process is performed for each number, and finally, the number of operations is returned. 

This algorithm effectively iterates and counts the complement pairs in linear time (O(n)), where n is the length of the input array.

