public int minSkips(int[] dist, int speed, int hoursBefore) {
    int n = dist.length;
    double[][] dp = new double[n + 1][n + 1];

    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            dp[i][j] = 1e9;
        }
    }
    dp[0][0] = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = -1; j < i; j++) {
            double t = dist[i] / (double)speed + (j != -1 ? 1 : 0);
            dp[i + 1][j + 1] = Math.min(dp[i + 1][j + 1], dp[i][j] + t);
            double skipped_t = Math.ceil(dp[i][j] + dist[i] / (double)speed) - dp[i][j];
            dp[i + 1][j] = Math.min(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j] + skipped_t);
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        if (dp[n][i] <= hoursBefore) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

    We will use dynamic programming to solve this problem. Our approach is to create a 2D DP array `dp`, where `dp[i][j]` will store the minimum time to reach road `i` by skipping `j` rest times.

1. Initialize the DP array `dp` with a very large value (1e9) for all `i` and `j`, and set `dp[0][0]` to `0`, because initially we are at the starting point and have not skipped any rests.
2. Iterate through all the roads `i` from `0` to `n-1`. For each road `i`, iterate through the number of rests skipped `j` from `-1` to `i-1` (inclusive).
    1. Calculate the time `t` to travel the current road, taking into consideration whether we should wait for the next integer hour or not after completing the previous road. If `j` is equal to `-1`, there is no wait needed, otherwise there is a wait of `1` hour.
    2. Update `dp[i+1][j+1]` by taking the minimum of its current value and the sum of `dp[i][j]` and `t`.
    3. Calculate the time `skipped_t` to travel the current road if we skip the rest, which is equal to the ceiling of the sum of `dp[i][j]` and the travel time of the current road minus `dp[i][j]`.
    4. Update `dp[i+1][j]` by taking the minimum of its current value and the sum of `dp[i][j]` and `skipped_t`.
3. Iterate through the DP array `dp[n][i]` for all `i`, and find the minimum number of skips required to arrive at the meeting on time. If the time is less than or equal to `hoursBefore`, return `i`. If not found, return `-1`.

Time complexity of this approach is O(n^2), and space complexity is also O(n^2) due to the DP array.

