public int minOperations(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
    int sum1 = Arrays.stream(nums1).sum();
    int sum2 = Arrays.stream(nums2).sum();
    if (sum1 == sum2) return 0;
    if (sum1 < sum2) {
        int[] temp = nums1;
        nums1 = nums2;
        nums2 = temp;
    }
    Arrays.sort(nums1);
    Arrays.sort(nums2);
    int diff = Math.abs(sum1 - sum2), operations = 0;
    int i = nums1.length - 1, j = 0;
    while (diff > 0 && (i >= 0 || j < nums2.length)) {
        int option1 = i >= 0 ? nums1[i] - 1 : 0;
        int option2 = j < nums2.length ? 6 - nums2[j] : 0;
        if (option1 > option2) {
            diff -= option1;
            i--;
        } else {
            diff -= option2;
            j++;
        }
        operations++;
    }
    return diff == 0 ? operations : -1;
}
```


    1. Calculate the sum of both the arrays. If the sums are already equal, return 0.
2. Determine which array has a greater sum. Sort the greater array in descending order and the smaller array in ascending order.
3. Initialize pointers `i` and `j` to start at the beginning of the greater and smaller arrays respectively.
4. While the difference between the sums is more than 0 and there are still elements in either array, evaluate the "options" for changing an element in each array.
    - For the greater sum array, check how much we can change the current element (i.e. subtract the minimum possible value, 1). If there are no more elements, set this option to 0.
    - For the smaller sum array, check how much we can change the current element (i.e. add the maximum possible difference, 6 minus the current value). If there are no more elements, set this option to 0.
5. Choose the option with the greatest change and decrement the difference by that change. Move the corresponding pointer position.
6. Increment the number of operations.
7. If the final difference is 0, return the operations count. Otherwise, return -1.

