import java.util.*;

class MovieRentingSystem {
    private Map<Integer, Map<Integer, Integer>> price;
    private TreeSet<int[]> rented;
    private List<TreeSet<int[]>> available;

    public MovieRentingSystem(int n, int[][] entries) {
        price = new HashMap<>();
        rented = new TreeSet<>((a, b) -> a[0] == b[0] ? a[1] - b[1] : a[0] - b[0]);
        available = new ArrayList<>(10000);
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
            available.add(new TreeSet<>((a, b) -> a[0] == b[0] ? a[1] - b[1] : a[0] - b[0]));
        }

        for (int[] entry : entries) {
            int shop = entry[0], movie = entry[1], p = entry[2];
            price.computeIfAbsent(shop, x -> new HashMap<>()).put(movie, p);
            available.get(movie).add(new int[]{p, shop});
        }
    }

    public List<Integer> search(int movie) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Iterator<int[]> iterator = available.get(movie).iterator();
        for (int i = 0; i < 5 && iterator.hasNext(); i++) {
            result.add(iterator.next()[1]);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public void rent(int shop, int movie) {
        int p = price.get(shop).get(movie);
        rented.add(new int[]{p, shop});
        available.get(movie).remove(new int[]{p, shop});
    }

    public void drop(int shop, int movie) {
        int p = price.get(shop).get(movie);
        rented.remove(new int[]{p, shop});
        available.get(movie).add(new int[]{p, shop});
    }

    public List<List<Integer>> report() {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Iterator<int[]> iterator = rented.iterator();
        for (int i = 0; i < 5 && iterator.hasNext(); i++) {
            int[] pair = iterator.next();
            result.add(Arrays.asList(pair[1], pair[0]));
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```


    The algorithm maintains three data structures to store the renting system information:
1. `price` - a nested hashmap/dictionary/map to store the rental prices of the movies.
2. `rented` - a set of movie-shop pairs currently rented, sorted by price and then by shop ID.
3. `available` - an array of sets, indexed by movie ID, storing available movie copies in each set sorted by price and shop ID.

The class constructor initializes and populates the above data structures.

The `search()` method finds available copies of the requested movie, and returns the top 5 shops based on price and shop ID.

The `rent()` method moves a movie-shop pair from the available set to the rented set.

The `drop()` method moves a movie-shop pair from the rented set back to the available set.

The `report()` method returns a list of the 5 cheapest rented movie-shop pairs.

By using these data structures, we can achieve efficient insertion, removal, and searching operations, while keeping track of rented and available (unrented) movie-shop pairs.

