import java.util.*;

public int minPartitionDifference(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length / 2;
    Arrays.sort(nums);
    int firstSum = 0, secondSum = 0;
    for (int i = nums.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (i % 2 == 0) {
            firstSum += nums[i];
        } else {
            secondSum += nums[i];
        }
    }
    return Math.abs(firstSum - secondSum);
}
```

    The algorithm for this problem consists of the following steps:

1. Sort the input array, nums, in descending order.
2. Divide the sorted array, nums, into two parts of equal length (n).
3. Calculate the sum of the first half of nums.
4. Calculate the sum of the second half of nums.
5. Return the absolute difference between the sums of the two halves.

The reason why sorting the input array works is because the largest difference between two numbers can be minimized by pairing them with the smallest numbers. Sorting the array from largest to smallest ensures that the largest numbers are paired with the smallest ones. Then we divide the array into two parts and find the absolute difference between their sums. That's the minimum possible absolute difference for the given input array.

