public int maxScore(int[] scores, int[][] edges) {
    ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj_list = new ArrayList<>(scores.length);
    for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
        adj_list.add(new ArrayList<>());
    }

    for (int[] edge : edges) {
        adj_list.get(edge[0]).add(edge[1]);
        adj_list.get(edge[1]).add(edge[0]);
    }

    int result = -1;
    for (int[] edge : edges) {
        for (int neighbor : adj_list.get(edge[0])) {
            for (int next : adj_list.get(edge[1])) {
                if (next != neighbor && next != edge[0]) {
                    result = Math.max(result, scores[edge[0]] + scores[edge[1]] + scores[neighbor] + scores[next]);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```


    The algorithm first constructs an adjacency list from the given edges. An adjacency list is a data structure where each node is associated with a list of its neighboring nodes. Then, for each edge in the given graph, it checks all possible combinations of reachable nodes and calculates the sum of their scores, updating the result with the maximum score found. If no valid combination is found, the function returns -1 as specified.

