public String smallestWordForLCP(int[][] lcp) {
    int n = lcp.length;
    char[] word = new char[n];
    Arrays.fill(word, 'a');
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i)
        for (int j = 1; j < n; ++j) {
            if (lcp[i][j] > Math.min(lcp[i - 1][j], lcp[i][j - 1])) {
                word[i + lcp[i][j] - 1] = (char) Math.max(word[i + lcp[i][j] - 1], word[i - 1] + 1);
            } else if (lcp[i][j] < Math.min(lcp[i - 1][j], lcp[i][j - 1])) {
                return "";
            }
        }
    return new String(word);
}
```

    The algorithm loops through the `lcp` matrix, checking for each element if it is greater or less than the minimum of its top and left neighbors. If greater, it updates the corresponding character in the `word` array or string by taking the maximum of its current value and the previous character increased by 1. If the element is less than the minimum of its top and left neighbors, we return empty string because it's an invalid `lcp` matrix.

After the loop, the function returns the word as a string. The algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2) and space complexity of O(n).

